# hashmap - a dynamically growing hash map mapped onto tidwall/hashmap.c.
#
# Two monomorphic shapes form the requirement baseline, matching std::map's
# pair: `HashStrInt` (char * key -> int value) and `HashIntPtr` (int key ->
# borrowed void * value). Each shape owns a distinct wrapper struct
# (`struct ct_hashmap_strint` / `struct ct_hashmap_intptr`), so the two CT hosts
# never collide in the mapping table even though both wrap `struct hashmap *`.
#
# Ownership: an item is copied into the table. A `char *` key is deep-copied by
# the shim (strdup + elfree), a pointer value is copied as a pointer only; the
# pointee stays with the caller.
#
# `third_party/` (not `vendor/`) holds the upstream hashmap.c / hashmap.h,
# pinned at commit 3735986914fc1c4e6a9123b406d0970666aa4aec with checksums in
# tools/third_party.sha256. The name matters: `ct get` skips a directory named
# `vendor`, so a package shipping its own would install without it. `cflags` /
# `ldflags` are written from the consumer root, where this package lands as
# `vendor/hashmap/`, and are exercised by the install section of tools/check.sh.
#
# Released: `version = 0.1.0` is the first release; the lightweight tag
# `v0.1.0` points at the release commit. Index metadata (`index/tools/repos.txt`
# and the `index/packages.txt` line) is refreshed by the index owner separately.
name = hashmap
version = 0.1.0
licence = MIT
cflags = -I vendor/hashmap/third_party
ldflags = vendor/hashmap/third_party/hashmap.c
requires = ct >= 0.20.3
